# Operator task injection from the dashboard — FR-DASH-5 (docs/PRD.md:526-534).

# 'prospect' | 'excavate' | 'haul'. Anything else is rejected outright
# (MANUAL_TASK_CAPABILITIES, orchestrator_node.py:112-116, tested at :414).
string task_type

# Target in world metres. z is unused.
geometry_msgs/Point target_location

# KILOGRAMS. Meaning depends on task_type:
#   excavate -- target extracted mass for this task
#   haul     -- target delivered mass for this task
#   prospect -- IGNORED; a survey has no mass. A non-zero value is accepted and
#               the response message says it was ignored.
# 0.0 means unconstrained: fill to the robot's own RCDL capacity, which is the
# behaviour before this field was read at all. Negative or non-finite is
# rejected. NOT clamped here — the orchestrator has no HAL and no RCDL; the
# agent clamps against selene_hal/config/<type>.yaml (excavator hopper_fill
# capacity_kg 20, hauler load_cell capacity_kg 50).
float32 quantity

# OPTIONAL, and a PREFERENCE — not a force-assignment.
# The task enters the auction like any other (docs/PRD.md:533, FR-DASH-5(b)).
# If this robot bids it wins regardless of score; if it does not bid within
# inject_preferred_robot_max_rounds auctions the preference is dropped with a
# WARNING alert and the auction opens up.
# It is NOT a way to pre-empt a busy robot. Use OverrideRobot 'cancel_task'
# first — that path is logged and appears in the task history, and it works: the
# old force-assign path published a TaskAssignment that agent_node.py:682-688
# discards for any robot not in BIDDING or ASSIGNED, i.e. exactly the busy robot
# it was meant to serve, after having already re-PENDed that robot's task.
string assigned_robot_id

# EMERGENCY. A DELIBERATE CHANGE TO AUCTION SEMANTICS, not a defect fix.
#
# True asks the orchestrator to ABORT an auction that is already in flight for
# a DIFFERENT, LOWER-priority task and open this one in the same tick
# (task_feed.should_preempt, orchestrator_node._preempt_for_emergency). The
# aborted task is restored to the status it held before that auction started,
# its round is refunded, and it is re-announced on a later tick.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN.
#   * It does NOT force-assign. The emergency task still enters the auction and
#     still has to attract a bid -- everything the assigned_robot_id comment
#     above says about preference-not-assignment applies unchanged.
#   * It does NOT free a busy robot. A robot mid-task is still mid-task; the
#     preemption ends an AUCTION, not an ASSIGNMENT. Use OverrideRobot
#     'cancel_task' for that, exactly as before.
#   * It does NOT change priority. Priority is still 10.0 for every operator
#     injection, emergency or not, and priority alone still does NOT preempt:
#     a non-emergency priority-10 injection waits for the in-flight auction to
#     resolve, which is the pre-existing behaviour and is deliberately kept so
#     that "urgent" remains something the operator says rather than something
#     the orchestrator infers.
#   * It does NOT preempt another emergency at the same priority. Equal
#     priority never preempts, which is what makes the mechanism terminate
#     rather than letting two injections abort each other's auctions forever.
#   * It does NOT grant a standing right to abort auctions. It buys exactly ONE
#     abort, spent only on a tick that goes on to announce this task in the
#     same pass -- so an emergency no idle robot is capable of, or one the D-06
#     ledger will not let be announced, aborts nothing at all rather than
#     aborting a fresh auction every time any robot anywhere finishes a task.
#     See task_queue.TaskEntry.preemption_spent.
#
# Appended at the end, before the '---', so the wire layout of every existing
# request field is unchanged. False is the default a ROS 2 bool takes, and
# false is exactly today's behaviour, so an older client that does not set it
# gets what it has always got.
bool emergency
---
bool success
string task_id
string message
